  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "In many languages the everyday word is a local short form of television, said as a word. English uses TV, said as two letter names, in speech and writing alike, and it is not informal.",
    "grammar": "Countable for the machine (a TV, two TVs) and uncountable for the programs (watch TV, on TV, too much TV). The verb is watch, not see or look at. It is on TV, not in TV.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Two letter names, tee-vee, with the stress on vee; before a noun, as in TV show, the stress moves to the first part."
  },
